<Base title="Questions and limits · Wörgl" description="What people ask about Wörgl, a hackathon project on NEAR testnet, and what it does not do." active="/faq">
<section class="section lead-section" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <h1 id="faq-title" class="page-title">Questions people ask</h1>
  <div class="faq">
    <details open><summary>What do NEAR, testnet and DAO mean?</summary><p><b>NEAR</b> is a blockchain, a shared record no single company controls. <b>Testnet</b> is its practice version, where nothing costs or is worth real money. A <b>DAO</b> decides by voting on a blockchain. Any DAO, with any approval rule, can govern a Wörgl token. The demo uses a small one with three members, where two must agree.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is this a memecoin?</summary><p>It was built for the memecoin track of NEAR HACKATHON, but it is not for speculating. There is no price and no way to buy coins. The meme is the idea: money that expires.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why would anyone want money that shrinks?</summary><p>Because the loss is small and goes back to the community. If holding costs a little, a coin is more useful spent than kept. It suits budgets, grants and event credits, which are meant to be used.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Who decides who gets paid?</summary><p>The governor, which is chosen at deployment and is usually a DAO. The contract enforces a cap of 10% of the pot per day, whatever the governor decides. The governor cannot touch anyone's balance or change the melt.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I send everything I have?</summary><p>Yes. The <b>Max</b> button uses a separate contract call, <code>transfer_all</code>. A balance melts every second, so a typed amount could be a little too large by the time it lands. <code>transfer_all</code> moves every share you hold, so you end at exactly zero and nothing is left behind. The page shows the amount the transaction reports. The receiver must be registered, and you cannot send to yourself.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can I give coins back to the treasury?</summary><p>Yes. Send them to the token account itself (<code>worgl-demo.testnet</code>), with Send or Max. Those coins are removed from your balance and the treasury grows by the same amount. A grant that was not needed can go back this way. It does not raise today's payout cap until the next UTC day.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why is there a claim button?</summary><p>For testing on testnet, so visitors can try Wörgl without asking anyone for coins. A real DAO would not have an open claim. It would pay members through proposals its governor approves.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can the test faucet be cheated?</summary><p>A little. Accounts are free, so one person can claim many times. The faucet has no limit of its own and is separate from payouts. It exists only for testing and would not be in a real deployment. That is one more reason a real deployment would pay out through the DAO.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What happens when the demo runs out?</summary><p>This copy melts fast on purpose. It stays accurate for about <span data-accurate>4.5 months</span>. After that, large payouts fail, then claims stop, and finally every balance reads zero. The numbers page shows the exact dates. At the real rate of 1% a month it lasts for centuries.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
  <h2 id="limits-title">Good to know</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><b>This is a hackathon project on testnet.</b> The coins have no value, and the code has not been audited.</li>
    <li><b>The demo runs at a fast speed.</b> 1% every <span data-period>hour</span>, so you can watch it. The real setting is 1% a month. A month here means 30 days.</li>
    <li><b>The test faucet is open to everyone.</b> Accounts are free, so one person can claim many times. A real DAO would pay out through approved proposals instead.</li>
    <li><b>The governor chooses who is paid.</b> The contract enforces the daily cap; whoever governs the token decides the recipients.</li>
    <li><b>Coins are not yet usable inside other contracts.</b> The token does not support <code>ft_transfer_call</code>. Trading pools and other contracts that record a deposited amount are not supported, because balances keep melting.</li>
    <li id="key-limit"><b>The contract can still be replaced.</b> <span id="key-status">Checking this account's keys…</span></li>
    <li><b>Balances change with the clock.</b> Wallets and explorers show the new balance when they read it again.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
